"},{"label":"C","text":"acute cardiac failure due to metastases in the myocardium of the (L) heart ventricle"},{"label":"D","text":"severe anaemia"},{"label":"E","text":"shock due to acute blood loss e.g. in a malignant stomach tumor"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-41b04e152725","stem":"Mark the wrong statement. The diagnosis “well differentiated mucinous adenocarcinoma” corresponds with the following histological findings","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"atypical glandular epithelial cells"},{"label":"B","text":"excessive keratin production"},{"label":"C","text":"excessive production of mucin"},{"label":"D","text":"relatively few mitoses"},{"label":"E","text":"tubular-glandular arrangement of cells"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9670cbb6f888","stem":"Mark the correct statement.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A complete carcinogen is a substance which can cause reversible mutations in the DNA of target cells"},{"label":"B","text":"The effect of a carcinogen is not dose dependant"},{"label":"C","text":"Aflatoxin is one of the strongest known complete carcinogens"},{"label":"D","text":"An oncogene is a part on the DNA responsible for coding the lack of a suppressor gene."},{"label":"E","text":"An oncogene is an incomplete carcinogen"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"793614997c187d73bb5c286c0b50dd2d5ca56fca"},{"id":"q-1070971b8523","stem":"Mark the correct statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A thrombus is intravitally formed"},{"label":"B","text":"Thrombi are always seen in the cardiac cavities post mortem"},{"label":"C","text":"Cruor is another name for a thrombus."},{"label":"D","text":"A thrombus is an aggregation of neutrophils and fibrin"},{"label":"E","text":"Thrombosis always causes pains in the particular tissue area"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"793614997c187d73bb5c286c0b50dd2d5ca56fca"},{"id":"q-20ecc0c13648","stem":"Mark the correct statement: fulminant pulmonal thrombembolism","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"always causes lung infarction"},{"label":"B","text":"causes a (L) heart failure"},{"label":"C","text":"causes chronic congestion in the liver"},{"label":"D","text":"causes acute ® heart failure"},{"label":"E","text":"causes ascites and pleural effusion"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a394f3151b71c377cd935c638ca38d274da01323"},{"id":"q-3144f261ad96","stem":"Mark the wrong statement: a thrombus","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"is covered with endothelium after a few days"},{"label":"B","text":"is macroscopically and microscopically not distinguishable from a post mortal clot"},{"label":"C","text":"always has contact to the inner vascular wall"},{"label":"D","text":"can be secondary infected"},{"label":"E","text":"can develop into a phlebolith"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bd47587a83ff","stem":"Mark the wrong statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"