 therapy?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Neoplasia","topicOriginal":"Neoplasia","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Physostigmine"},{"label":"B","text":"Allopurinol"},{"label":"C","text":"Aminocaproic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"Aminoglutethimide"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Neoplasia","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3f796dc8cf3b","stem":"Which of the following enzymes improves GIT functions (replacement therapy)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pepsin"},{"label":"B","text":"Urokinase"},{"label":"C","text":"L-asparaginase"},{"label":"D","text":"Lydaze"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-58a42015023f","stem":"Which of the following enzymes has fibrinolytic activity?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pepsin"},{"label":"B","text":"Urokinase"},{"label":"C","text":"L-asparaginase"},{"label":"D","text":"Lydaze"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fa3cc8e84d62","stem":"Which of the following enzymes is used in cancer therapy?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Neoplasia","topicOriginal":"Neoplasia","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pepsin"},{"label":"B","text":"Urokinase"},{"label":"C","text":"L-asparaginase"},{"label":"D","text":"Lydaze"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Neoplasia","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-65d6aa7d5564","stem":"Which of the following statements concerning nutritional supplement (dietary supplement) are True: 110","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nutritional supplements are intended to supplement the diet and bear or contain one or more of the following dietary ingredients: a vitamin, a mineral, an herb or other botanical, an amino acid, a dietary substance for human use to supplement the diet by increasing the total daily intake (e.g., enzymes or tissue from organ or glands), a concentrate, such as a meal replacement or energy bar, a metabolite, constituent, or extract"},{"label":"B","text":"Nutritional supplements are regulated as foods, and not as drugs"},{"label":"C","text":"Nutritional supplements are not pre-approved on their safety and efficacy, unlike drugs"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above PART VII Antihyperlipidemic Drugs & Drugs Used In the Treatment of Gout"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-853e7d0913b7","stem":"All of the following statements concerning drugs which inhibit cholesterol synthesis are true, EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"When used alone, they are the most effective agents for lowering LDL cholesterol"},{"label":"B","text":"They are often effective in patients in whom a diet, with or without a bile acid-binding resin or niacin, has failed"},{"label":"C","text":"Lovastatin (Mevacor) plus a resin causes regression of coronary lesions in about one third of treated patients 112"},{"label":"D","text":"Members of this drug class are generally not as well tolerated as the older bile acid-binding resins"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"392ec85d1793b06458b60186ea7b5a22ee193680"},{"id":"q-ff57361d9c97","stem":"When used alone, the bile acid-binding resins are contraindicated in patients with hypertriglyceridemia. This consideration is: 113","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1032ed590f26","stem":"Characteristics of probenecid include all of the following, EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It promotes the renal tubular secretion of penicillin"},{"label":"B","text":"It is useful in the treatment of gout"},{"label":"C","text":"At appropriate doses, it promotes the excretion of uric acid"},{"label":"D","text":"The metabolic products of probenecid are uricosuric PART VIII Agents That Affect Bone Mineral Homeostasis"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"73d2bd8e4ee30aa58390c5df12b6d5a2917177a1"},{"id":"q-82f585618ba9","stem":"The following statements about the parathyroid hormone are true, EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Endocrinology","topicOriginal":"Endocrinology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The parathyroid hormone (PTH) is a single-chain peptide hormone composed of 84 amino acids"},{"label":"B","text":"The parathyroid hormone increases calcium and phosphate absorption in intestine (by increased 1,25- dihydroxyvitamin D3 production)"},{"label":"C","text":"The parathyroid hormone increases serum calcium and decreases serum phosphate"},{"label":"D","text":"The parathyroid hormone increases calcium excretion and decreases phosphate excretion in kidneys"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Endocrinology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5ff3ebd975f0","stem":"Which of the following statements about calcitonin is true","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcitonin secreted by parafollicular cells of the mammalian thyroid is a single-chain peptide hormone with 32 amino acids"},{"label":"B","text":"Effects of calcitonin are to lower serum calcium and phosphate by acting on bones and kidneys."},{"label":"C","text":"Calcitonin inhibits osteoclastic bone resorption."},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8b3a305afc2b","stem":"Indications for calcitonin administration are the following, EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypercalcemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Paget's disease"},{"label":"C","text":"Hypophosphatemia"},{"label":"D","text":"Osteoporosis"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-74754bb422c0","stem":"Indications for 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3 (calcitriol) administration are the following, EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypocalcemia in chronic renal failure"},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin D-dependent rickets"},{"label":"C","text":"Malabsorption of vitamin D from intestine"},{"label":"D","text":"Elevated skeletal turnover"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2085b70f7304","stem":"The following statement refers to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3 (calcitriol)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"When rapidity of action is required, 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3 (calcitriol), 0.25-1 μg daily, is the vitamin D metabolite of choice, since it is capable of raising serum calcium within 24-48 hours"},{"label":"B","text":"Calcitriol also raises serum phosphate, though this action is usually not observed early in treatment"},{"label":"C","text":"Undergoes enterohepatic circulation"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eac1988d199b","stem":"Which of the following statements refers to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3 (calcitriol)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The combined effect of calcitriol and all other vitamin D metabolites and analogs on both calcium and phosphate makes careful monitoring of the level of these minerals especially important to avoid ectopic calcification"},{"label":"B","text":"Does not undergo enterohepatic circulation"},{"label":"C","text":"Toxic to osteoclasts"},{"label":"D","text":"Bioavailability increases with the administered dose"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fd3220a549bc","stem":"Which of the following statements refers to cholecalciferol","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Frequent monitoring of both calcium and phosphorus serum levels is necessary in case of intravenous administration"},{"label":"B","text":"Has potent anti-osteoclast activity – mechanism unknown"},{"label":"C","text":"Can usually lower serum calcium levels in 48 hours"},{"label":"D","text":"Mechanism of action: 1. Genomic effects 2. Cytoplasmic effects"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-18da68c83fb6","stem":"Correct statements about pamidronate include all of the following, EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Because it causes gastric irritation, pamidronate is not available as an oral preparation"},{"label":"B","text":"Skeletal half-life is 24 h"},{"label":"C","text":"Fever and lymphocytopenia are reversible"},{"label":"D","text":"Can be irritable to the esophagus if not washed promptly to the stomach"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5997bb269308","stem":"Correct statements about alendronate include all of the following, EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be irritable to the esophagus if not washed promptly to the stomach"},{"label":"B","text":"1st generation biphosphonate"},{"label":"C","text":"Reduces osteoclast activity without significantly affecting osteoblasts; useful in the treatment of Paget's disease"},{"label":"D","text":"More potent than EHDP; has a wider therapeutic window"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-59ee0fe4b75a","stem":"Indications of alendronate are the following, EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypoparathyroidism"},{"label":"B","text":"Glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Paget's disease"},{"label":"D","text":"Syndromes of ectopic calcification"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9959f97c81f7","stem":"Indications for etidronate administration are the following, EXEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paget's disease"},{"label":"B","text":"Osteoporosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Hypophosphatemia"},{"label":"D","text":"Hypercalcemia"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1a2531de1298","stem":"Which of the following statements refers to etidronate","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduces osteoclast activity without significantly affecting osteoblasts; useful in treatment of Paget's disease"},{"label":"B","text":"Serum phosphorus concentrations should be monitored at least daily in case of oral administration"},{"label":"C","text":"2nd generation biphosphonate (amino-biphosphonate)"},{"label":"D","text":"Bioavailability increases with the administered dose"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3232f897c53b","stem":"Correct statements about etidronate include all of the following, EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skeletal half-life is hundreds of days"},{"label":"B","text":"Bioavailability increases with the administered dose"},{"label":"C","text":"2nd generation biphosphonate (amino-biphosphonate)"},{"label":"D","text":"1st generation biphosphonate."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f18cb23a2fc4","stem":"The major causes of hypercalcemia in the adult are the following, EXCEPT : 119","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperparathyroidism"},{"label":"B","text":"Cancer with or without bone metastases"},{"label":"C","text":"Renal failure and malabsorption"},{"label":"D","text":"Hypervitaminosis D"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacolog