verified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-62254fab5a2b","stem":"Link the following Write the number on the right hand column alongside the alphabetical number on the left","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"koilonychia 2 1 rheumatoid arthritis"},{"label":"B","text":"clubbing 4 2 Fe deficiency"},{"label":"C","text":"Oslers nodes 3 3 infective endocarditis"},{"label":"D","text":"spindle fingers 4 cancer of the lung"},{"label":"E","text":"claw hand 5 cirrhosis f) leukonychia 5 6 osteoarthritis g) pitted nails 8 7 ulnar nerve lesion h) sclerodactyly 10 8 psoriasis i) Heberden’s nodes 6 9 gout j) tophus 9 10 scleroderma"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"58f08c9170fd1814825ad70b1e94c15d2d1b352f"},{"id":"q-73547f49024b","stem":"The following symptoms and signs are all consistent with congestive cardiac failure, except oedema, raised JVP, tender hepatomegaly, orthopnoea ,pallor, persisting retrosternal chest pain relieved by sitting forward, sacral swelling, basal crepitations on auscultation, bronchial breathing on auscultation, splinter haemorrhages in the nails","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f7b6ffe6ab2b","stem":"Clinical examination of a 20 year old women reveals: pulse rate 70/min,irregularly irregular, BP 90/70,JVP not raised, apex beat in the fifth intercostal space in mid clavicular line, left sternal edge has a prominent heave, increased pulsation in the second left intercostal space,diastolic thrill at the apex, loud first heart sound, mid-diastolic murmur,, increased pulmonary component of the second heart sound, normal breath sounds in the bases of the lung Which of the following possible diagnoses is consistent with these findings","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mixed mitral stenosis and mitral incompetence"},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral incompetence with atrial fibrillation and pulmonary hypertension"},{"label":"C","text":"Mitral stenosis with atrial fibrillatio n ,cardiomegaly and heart failure"},{"label":"D","text":"Mitral stenosis with pulmonary hypertension, right ventricular hypertrophy, and atrial fibrillation"},{"label":"E","text":"Aortic incompetence with right heart failure f) Mitral stenosis with right heart failure and ventricular ectopics g) Tricuspid incompetence with atrial fibrillation Select one"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"cb133592b361724af22762ac3368a2fd0cbd5fc1"},{"id":"q-5f8d2379d29a","stem":"A 22 year –old man had a 4 month history of malaise, weight loss, fevers and night sweats. Over the course of the past 4 weeks he had developed increasing breathlessness Physical examination showed a thin young man. Chest movements were diminished on the left, and trachea deviated to the right. The percussion note was dull on the left, where the breath sounds were almost absent, and both the vocal resonance and tactile fremitus were markedly reduced The XRay is shown as attached Answer the following questions","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"What is wrong with the lung?"},{"label":"B","text":"Why is the trachea deviated to the right?"},{"label":"C","text":"Why is the percussion note dull on the left?"},{"label":"D","text":"The fact that the vocal resonance and tactile fremitus are reduced rather than increased, excludes what type of pathology?"},{"label":"E","text":"What do you think is the diagnosis?"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-44c3820bc934","stem":"Draw a diagram of the visual pathway from the eyeball to the occiput.. Explain the mechanism of homonymous hemianopia and bitemporal hemianopia","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fccdd7d7c2a9","stem":"Write appropriate answers on the provided space for Short Notes Questions.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-551ce8315e8d","stem":"Write the number of each question at the top of each page.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-754bd42cc4f5","stem":"This is a Three Hours paper consisting of Fourteen printed pages with Four sections Section I: Multiple Choice Questions Section II: True/ False Questions Section III: Matching Questions Section IV: Short answer Questions","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2a83565813f9","stem":"Match the following drugs with the effects ...............inhibits the NaK2Cl transporter-mannitol ...............inhibits carbonic anhydrase-actazolamide ...............an antagonist of aldosterone-spirinolactone ...............inhibits the NaCl cotransporter in the distal tubule-thiazide diuretics ...............a freely filtered osmotically active substance which is not reabsorbed and opposes water reabsorption-mannitol","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Renal and Urinary","topicOriginal":"Renal and Urinary","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Renal and Urinary","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-2fdd8e39bd0e","stem":"Acute asthma treatment includes ...............oxygen ...............inhalaton of salbutamol in oxygen given by a nebulizer ...............intravenous hydrocortisone ...............Aminophylline tablets ...............antiviral drugs if bacterial infection is present.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Respiratory","topicOriginal":"Respiratory","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Respiratory","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-4e377385ee61","stem":"Omotic laxative ...............Magnesium sulphate ...............sodium sulphate ...............sodium potassium tartate ...............Sodium bicarbonate ...............aluminium hydroxide Matching Match the members of group A to those of group B by choosing and writing (in the space provided). The letter of the most corresponding member of group B. Members of group B shall be used only once and some shall nor be used at all. [pic]","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-3fc60d16512a","stem":"01–1 microgram/kg/min , titrated to perfusion/MAP. Dobutamine β1-predominant inotrope for low cardiac output when BP is adequate or supported. Typical infusion: 2–20 micrograms/kg/min . Risks: tachyarrhythmia, ischemia and hypotension. Milrinone PDE-3 inhibitor with inodilator effect. Typical infusion","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-647315de2231","stem":"5–5 mg daily 20–40 mg daily Same ACEi cautions. Candesartan 4–8 mg daily 32 mg daily Hyperkalemia/renal function; avoid pregnancy. Valsartan 40 mg BID 160 mg BID Hyperkalemia/renal function. Check BP, creatinine/eGFR and potassium after initiation and titration. A small stable creatinine rise can be hemodynamic and does not automatically require stopping therapy. Heart Failure · Ultra Detailed Evidence-based beta-blockers — exactly which ones The mortality-proven HFrEF beta-blockers are carvedilol, bisoprolol and metoprolol succinate . Do not assume all beta-blockers are interchangeable. Drug Starting Target Notes Carvedilol","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9eabb4110947","stem":"5–25 mg daily 200 mg daily Use succinate ER, not assuming metoprolol tartrate has equivalent HFrEF outcome evidence. When NOT to initiate/up-titrate Active cardiogenic shock, severe hypotension, symptomatic bradycardia/high-grade block, or markedly decompensated congestion requiring escalating inotropes. ACC 2024 specifically advises deferring beta-blocker initiation/titration until HF is compensated. Heart Failure · Ultra Detailed Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists Drug Dose Monitoring/adverse effects Spironolactone","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3f173c1092e6","stem":"Clinical presentation Early CKD is frequently asymptomatic, which is why the uploaded source stresses screening. Later features result from volume retention, toxin accumulation, anemia, acidosis and mineral-bone abnormalities. Symptoms Fatigue, weakness, poor exercise tolerance. Loss of appetite, nausea/vomiting, pruritus, sleep disturbance. Dyspnea from anemia or fluid overload. Edema and weight gain. Headache, dizziness, palpitations from anemia/HTN. Hiccups and other uremic symptoms in advanced disease. Bone pain/fractures in CKD-MBD. Signs Hypertension, including difficult-to-control BP. Pallor. Peripheral/sacral edema, raised JVP or pulmonary crackles when overloaded. Excoriations/pruritic skin changes. Neurologic changes in severe uremia. Signs of underlying systemic disease.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b32d64d02fcf","stem":"Who should be screened? Screen people at increased risk: diabetes, hypertension, cardiovascular disease, family history of kidney disease, HIV or other relevant chronic infections, recurrent AKI, nephrotoxic exposure, older age and known structural/urologic disease. At minimum obtain serum creatinine/eGFR and urine albumin assessment; urinalysis adds important etiologic information.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-79c82167022e","stem":"Major complications and mechanism Complication Mechanism Clinical consequence Volume overload/HTN Sodium/water retention, RAAS activation. Edema, pulmonary edema, LVH/HF. Hyperkalemia Reduced renal K excretion, worsened by acidosis/RAAS drugs. Potentially fatal arrhythmia. Metabolic acidosis Reduced acid excretion/bicarbonate regeneration. Muscle wasting, bone effects, dyspnea, progression. Anemia Low EPO + iron deficiency/inflammation/blood loss. Fatigue, dyspnea, cardiac strain. CKD-MBD PO4 retention, FGF23 rise, low calcitriol, secondary hyperparathyroidism. Bone disease/fracture, vascular calcification. Uremia Retention of toxins. Pericarditis, encephalopathy, nausea, pruritus, platelet dysfunction. Cardiovascular disease Traditional + CKD-specific risk. Major cause of death in CKD.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-eba05b0fddfb","stem":"Treatment — multidisciplinary and disease-modifying 1 Treat cause and reversible accelerants. Control diabetes/HTN, relieve obstruction, treat inflammatory/infectious disease, stop nephrotoxins such as unnecessary NSAIDs. 2 Reduce progression. BP control; ACEi/ARB particularly in albuminuric disease when tolerated; SGLT2 inhibitor in eligible CKD; optimize glycemia and cardiovascular risk. 3 Manage complications. Volume, potassium, acidosis, anemia, CKD-MBD, nutrition and symptoms. 4 Prepare for kidney failure early. Nephrology referral, modality education, vascular access/transplant work-up when indicated.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Renal and Urinary","topicOriginal":"Renal and Urinary","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Renal and Urinary","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d51deb5799b3","stem":"Anemia of CKD Do not assume every low Hb is “EPO deficiency.” Evaluate iron status, blood loss, B12/folate, inflammation and other causes. Correct iron deficiency first. ESA may be considered in selected patients after reversible causes are treated; avoid trying to normalize Hb completely because higher targets increase cardiovascular/thrombotic risk.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bc959b9bfe89","stem":"Definition AKI is an abrupt decline in kidney function. Current routine clinical diagnosis still uses KDIGO functional criteria: an increase in serum creatinine by ≥0.3 mg/dL (26.5 μmol/L) within 48 hours, or an increase to ≥1.5 times baseline known or presumed to have occurred within the prior 7 days, or urine volume <0.5 mL/kg/hour for 6 hours. Important update KDIGO has a 2026 AKI/AKD guideline draft under public review, but it is not yet the final standard. For exam-safe clinical definitions, the established KDIGO creatinine/urine-output criteria remain the key reference unless your institution explicitly adopts the new framework.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Renal and Urinary","topicOriginal":"Renal and Urinary","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Renal and Urinary","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-6236df13aa4a","stem":"Examination ABCDE and mental status. Volume: JVP, mucous membranes, capillary refill, BP including postural change when appropriate, edema, lung crackles. Perfusion/shock: temperature of extremities, pulse pressure, lactate context, urine output. Bladder distension and prostate/urologic clues. Rash, arthritis, purpura or systemic disease signs. Look actively for pulmonary edema, uremic encephalopathy and arrhythmia.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ddfc1d318cbf","stem":"Investigations Test Purpose Key findings Serial creatinine/urea Trend kidney function. Trajectory is more useful than one number. Strict urine output Diagnosis, staging and response. Oliguria identifies higher-risk phenotype. Na/K/HCO3/Mg/Ca/PO4 Life-threatening complications. Hyperkalemia, acidosis and other derangements. Urinalysis/microscopy Differentiate renal processes. Protein/hematuria/casts; muddy brown casts support tubular injury; RBC casts suggest GN. FBP, CRP/cultures as appropriate Sepsis/anemia/platelets. Etiology and procedure risk. CK Rhabdomyolysis when suspected. Markedly elevated CK. Renal/bladder ultrasound Exclude obstruction and assess kidneys. Hydronephrosis, bladder retention, kidney size. ECG Hyperkalemia/ischemia/arrhythmia. Peaked T waves, QRS widening etc if severe hyperkalemia.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-c5cca977255a","stem":"Immediate management principle 1 Treat the patient before the creatinine. ABCDE, recognize shock, hyperkalemia, pulmonary edema, severe acidosis and uremic complications. 2 Stop kidney insults. Hold unnecessary nephrotoxins and renally dose medications. 3 Identify the phenotype. Depleted, overloaded, septic, cardiogenic, obstructed, or intrinsic renal disease. 4 Correct cause. Restore perfusion when depleted, treat sepsis, relieve obstruction, treat GN/AIN/rhabdomyolysis as appropriate. 5 Monitor closely. Urine output, weight/fluid balance, electrolytes, acid-base status and creatinine trajectory.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Respiratory","topicOriginal":"Respiratory","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Respiratory","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c022b96dca3a","stem":"The fluid-versus-diuretic dilemma This is the central theme of the uploaded CME. AKI is not an automatic indication for IV fluids. First determine whether the patient is intravascularly depleted and fluid responsive. A patient with pulmonary edema and raised venous pressures can worsen with more fluid. Situation Approach True hypovolemia Careful isotonic crystalloid resuscitation with repeated reassessment. Septic shock Resuscitate according to perfusion; use vasopressors when needed rather than endless fluid. Cardiorenal/congested Decongestion may improve renal venous pressure; avoid reflex fluid challenge. Fluid-overloaded AKI Loop diuretics may treat overload if there is response; they do not “cure” AKI. Refractory overload despite diuretics Consider kidney replacement therapy when clinically significant.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Respiratory","topicOriginal":"Respiratory","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Respiratory","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ddf3b9e7a4de","stem":"Life-threatening complications The source highlights four major homeostatic failures: fluid overload, electrolyte imbalance such as hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, and uremia . These are also the classic triggers for urgent dialysis when refractory or severe.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0ba4ca30e572","stem":"Hyperkalemia in AKI ECG immediately in severe hyperkalemia. IV calcium if ECG changes/severe instability to stabilize myocardium. Insulin + dextrose, ± nebulized beta-agonist to shift potassium intracellularly. Bicarbonate mainly when significant metabolic acidosis is present. Remove potassium with diuresis/binders where suitable or dialysis if severe/refractory.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0067bf191e11","stem":"Kidney replacement therapy — when? AEIOU A cidosis refractory to medical treatment; E lectrolyte disturbance, especially severe/refractory hyperkalemia; selected I ntoxications; refractory fluid O verload; symptomatic U remia such as encephalopathy/pericarditis/bleeding. The uploaded source appropriately emphasizes that dialysis initiation is driven mainly by the patient's clinical presentation, not by an arbitrary creatinine threshold. Dialysis is supportive; unnecessary “early” dialysis can expose the patient to catheter complications, hypotension, arrhythmia and dialysis disequilibrium without improving recovery in patients who do not otherwise need it.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a0babaa14bcd","stem":"Modalities in AKI Modality Best suited to Intermittent hemodialysis (IHD) Hemodynamically stable patients needing rapid solute/fluid removal. Continuous kidney replacement therapy (CKRT/CRRT) Hemodynamically unstable ICU patients when available. Peritoneal dialysis Useful alternative, especially where extracorporeal resources are limited and in selected pediatric/resource-limited settings.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Renal and Urinary","topicOriginal":"Renal and Urinary","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Renal and Urinary","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8f5a8ca0e919","stem":"Definition Hemorrhagic stroke is acute neurologic dysfunction caused by intracranial bleeding. The major categories are intracerebral hemorrhage (ICH), where blood is within brain parenchyma, and subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH), where blood is in the subarachnoid space. This chapter focuses on spontaneous non-traumatic ICH.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Neurology","topicOriginal":"Neurology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Neurology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-330cdddb2743","stem":"Site-based clinical syndromes Site Typical presentation Basal ganglia/internal capsule Contralateral hemiparesis, facial weakness, sensory loss, gaze deviation. Thalamus Sensory loss, reduced consciousness, vertical gaze abnormality. Cerebellum Severe headache, vomiting, vertigo, ataxia; rapid deterioration from brainstem compression/hydrocephalus. Pons Coma, pinpoint pupils, quadriparesis, abnormal respiration. Lobar cortex Seizures, aphasia, neglect, visual-field deficit depending on lobe. Intraventricular extension Reduced GCS, hydrocephalus, raised ICP.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Neurology","topicOriginal":"Neurology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Neurology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ea82e917788f","stem":"Immediate assessment: do not start with a long clerking A Airway: assess protection. Call ICU/anesthesia early for GCS ≤8, recurrent vomiting, bulbar dysfunction, uncontrolled seizures, brainstem signs or worsening consciousness. B Breathing: RR, SpO2, pattern, aspiration. Give oxygen if hypoxemic. Avoid hypoxia and hypercapnia. C Circulation: two IV lines, BP, pulse/ECG, perfusion and urine output. Avoid hypotension; use isotonic rather than hypotonic fluid when needed. D Disability: GCS, pupils, limb power, speech, seizures, swallowing and immediate glucose. Hypoglycemia is a stroke mimic. E Exposure: trauma, fever, other bleeding, anticoagulants/antiplatelets, DVT/pressure injury risk.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-8ff510a30e42","stem":"CT brain — the key investigation Urgent non-contrast CT brain differentiates hemorrhage from ischemia rapidly. Acute blood appears hyperdense/bright. Every CT report or ward presentation should explicitly state: Location. Approximate volume. Intraventricular extension. Perihematomal edema. Mass effect. Midline shift. Hydrocephalus. Brainstem compression. Herniation features. Posterior fossa involvement.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Neurology","topicOriginal":"Neurology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Neurology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eb630bebc12e","stem":"Laboratory work-up Investigation Why Random glucose Exclude hypoglycemia and treat severe dysglycemia. FBP/platelets Hb, infection, thrombocytopenia and procedure safety. PT/INR Warfarin effect, liver disease, coagulopathy. aPTT Heparin effect/coagulation disorder. Urea/creatinine/electrolytes Renal function, Na/K, drug/reversal decisions. LFT Liver-related coagulopathy. Group/crossmatch If surgery/transfusion may be needed. ECG ± troponin AF, ischemia, arrhythmia and stroke-heart interactions. Toxicology / pregnancy test When clinically relevant.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-47ff8b6af2c0","stem":"Severity scores GCS is immediately useful for airway, monitoring and deterioration. ICH score incorporates GCS, age ≥80, infratentorial origin, hematoma volume ≥30 mL and intraventricular extension. A higher score predicts worse mortality but should not be used alone to limit life-sustaining treatment. What to say in viva “NIHSS can describe neurological deficit, but ICH score is more disease-specific for prognostic communication in intracerebral hemorrhage. CHA2DS2-VASc is relevant later only if AF is present and future anticoagulation is being reconsidered.”","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Neurology","topicOriginal":"Neurology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Neurology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-2d92717b8dc5","stem":"Stop and reverse antithrombotics Agent Urgent reversal principle Warfarin Stop; 4-factor PCC + IV vitamin K preferred where available. If PCC unavailable, IV vitamin K plus plasma-based replacement per local protocol. Unfractionated heparin Stop; protamine according to recent dose/exposure. Dabigatran Idarucizumab 5 g IV when available; dialysis may be relevant in selected severe accumulation/renal failure. Apixaban/rivaroxaban/other Xa inhibitor Andexanet alfa where available/appropriate; otherwise PCC-based strategies per protocol. Antiplatelets Stop acutely; routine platelet transfusion is not automatically indicated for every antiplatelet-associated ICH.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Renal and Urinary","topicOriginal":"Renal and Urinary","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Renal and Urinary","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c22cfa166969","stem":"Osmotherapy Use for clinical/radiologic raised ICP, not routinely in every ICH. The uploaded source lists mannitol 0.25–1 g/kg IV bolus and 3% hypertonic saline according to local protocol. Monitor sodium, osmolality, renal function, urine output and neurological response. Mannitol is problematic in severe hypotension/dehydration/renal failure unless specialist guided.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Renal and Urinary","topicOriginal":"Renal and Urinary","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Renal and Urinary","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8e699c8c9d97","stem":"Seizures Treat clinical or electrographic seizures. Do not give prophylactic antiseizure medication routinely to every ICH patient without seizures. For active convulsion the source lists IV benzodiazepine followed, when needed, by an agent such as levetiracetam or phenytoin according to protocol.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Neurology","topicOriginal":"Neurology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Neurology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5cb7649103e0","stem":"Secondary prevention Long-term BP control is the most important modifiable strategy. The uploaded source cites a target around 130/80 mmHg for recurrence prevention. Address adherence, smoking, heavy alcohol, diabetes, CKD progression, NSAID overuse and future anticoagulant decisions individually. Exam traps Do not give aspirin before CT excludes hemorrhage. Do not use CHA2DS2-VASc to manage acute ICH. Do not give prophylactic anticonvulsants to every ICH patient. Do not equate normal CSF production with a desired EVD drainage volume. Do not lower BP abruptly to “normal.” ← AKI All topics","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Endocrinology","topicOriginal":"Endocrinology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Endocrinology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-76ec3895def0","stem":"The absorption of which one of the following is greatly decreased in the absence of gastric acidity?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"flucytosine"},{"label":"B","text":"fluconazole"},{"label":"C","text":"nystatin"},{"label":"D","text":"ketaconazole"},{"label":"E","text":"zidovusine"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9474b8db8c72","stem":"Angeotensin converting enzyme inhibitors ............... treatment of a heart failure starts with a fraction of the target dose ...............Potassium and creatinine should be regularly measured in ACE- treated patients ...............ACE inhibitors should be combined with potassium-saving drugs ...............ACE inhibitors should be replaced with AT1 receptor blockers in patients who develop cough ...............ACE inhibitors can be combined with beta blockers","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-096d0fed86e7","stem":"The following are clinical uses of thiazide diuretics ...............Hypertension ...............Mild heart failure ...............Idiopathic hypercalciuria ...............Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus ...............Primary hyperaldosterism","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-8b1e5f65ce7b","stem":"The following drugs are bacteriostatic rather than bactericidal at doses normally used in clinical practice except: ............... Penicillins ............... Aminoglycoside antibiotics ............... Erythromycin ............... Cefuroxime ............... Ciprofloxacin","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Infectious Diseases","topicOriginal":"Infectious Diseases","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Infectious Diseases","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-bc11b0c34a65","stem":"Angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor ...............reduce preload ...............are positive inotropes ...............do not cause cough ...............no effect on afer load ...............causes muscles fasiculation 23 Which of the following induces microsomal enzymes ...............Phenobarbitone ...............Ethanol ...............Diazepam ...............Ketaconazole ...............Nevirapine","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-52bae9ac3008","stem":"This is a TWO Hours paper consisting of.............. pages with THREE sections Section I: Multiple Choice Questions Section II: True/ False Questions (Penalty questions) Section lll: Matching and Filling the Gaps Questions","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"